        "For When should a BDR team use a round-robin sender model in the next review cycle, start with the decision the record must support and the person who will review it. Share the aggregate results with the team and invite questions. If one sender consistently receives harder accounts, adjust the weighting or the rules. The measure should support a conversation about fairness, not become a leaderboard. If the same question appears twice, fix the template rather than the answer.",
        "The useful version of When should a BDR team use a round-robin sender model in the next review cycle fits on one page and names the owner, the source, and the next date. A simple table of assigned leads, completed touches, and substantive conversations by sender is usually enough. Add a note about account difficulty so a low count is not read as low effort. Discuss the table as a team rather than publishing a ranking. Set a review date now so the following cycle has a real checkpoint."
